{% youtube src="https://youtu.be/0R55-uFQyX4" title="Task-based affected in Nx" /%}

[Remote caching](/docs/features/ci-features/remote-cache) speeds up your CI, but you can go even faster by skipping unnecessary tasks altogether.

Most pull requests change a few files, so CI should only run the tasks those files affect.
`nx affected` selects and runs only the tasks that need to run for the given changes.

For example, say a `web` app depends on a `ui` library, and your pull request only updates the unit test file `button.spec.ts` in `ui`.
`nx affected` runs `ui:test` and skips `ui:build`, since spec files aren't an input to the [production](/docs/reference/inputs#production-inputs) build.
It skips `web:build` and `web:test` too, because the spec file for `ui` isn't an input to either.

![Changing button.spec.ts in ui runs ui:test and skips ui:build, web:build, and web:test](../../../../assets/features/ci-features/affected-tasks.svg)

## How Nx selects affected tasks

Run `nx affected` with the targets you want, particularly on CI:

```shell
nx affected -t <targets>
```

For the `web` and `ui` example, run the affected `build` and `test` tasks:

```shell
nx affected -t build test
```

Nx then:

1. Uses Git to find the files your branch changed, for example `libs/ui/src/button.spec.ts`.
1. Selects each `build` and `test` task whose [inputs](/docs/reference/inputs) include a changed file.
1. Selects the tasks that [read the outputs](/docs/reference/inputs#outputs-of-dependent-tasks) of a selected task, like a `web:build` that reads the `ui` build output.
1. Runs the selected tasks and the tasks they depend on, restoring unchanged dependencies from the cache.

Only `ui:test` reads the spec file. In this `nx.json`, `production` is a [named input](/docs/reference/inputs#named-inputs) that leaves out spec files, and `^` reads an input from a project's dependencies:

```json
// nx.json
{
  "namedInputs": {
    "production": ["{projectRoot}/**/*", "!{projectRoot}/**/*.spec.ts"]
  },
  "targetDefaults": {
    "build": {
      "inputs": ["production", "^production"]
    },
    "test": {
      "inputs": ["{projectRoot}/**/*", "^production"]
    }
  }
}
```

To see the affected tasks in the task graph without running them, pass `--graph`:

```shell
nx affected -t build test --graph
```

{% aside type="note" title="What decides whether a task is affected" %}
Nx selects a task when your change touches one of its inputs, not by the task hierarchy in the task graph.

**`dependsOn` alone doesn't make a task affected.** `web:build` depends on `ui:build`, but a change affects `web:build` only through its own inputs, such as `^production`, or the `ui` build output read through [`dependentTasksOutputFiles`](/docs/reference/inputs#outputs-of-dependent-tasks) or an [`includeIgnored`](/docs/reference/inputs#gitignored-files) fileset. An edge that only orders the two tasks doesn't carry the change across.

**Declare inputs and outputs, even for tasks you don't cache.** Nx selects tasks from them. If `web:test` lists only `{projectRoot}/**/*`, a change to the `ui` source doesn't select it, even though `web` imports `ui`. If `ui:build` writes to `dist` without listing it in `outputs`, a `web:build` that reads that folder isn't selected when the `ui` build changes.

**[Sync generators](/docs/concepts/sync-generators) that change files during a run don't update the selection.** Nx selects tasks before syncing, so run `nx sync` first, or run the command again after a sync applies changes.
{% /aside %}

## Configure affected on CI

The affected command takes a `base` and `head` commit. The default `base` is your `main` branch, and the default `head` is your current file system. This is generally what you want when developing locally, but in CI, you need to customize these values.

```shell
nx affected -t build --base=origin/main --head=$PR_BRANCH_NAME # where PR_BRANCH_NAME is defined by your CI system
nx affected -t build --base=origin/main~1 --head=origin/main # rerun what is affected by the last commit in main
```

You can also set the base and head SHAs as environment variables:

```shell
NX_BASE=origin/main~1
NX_HEAD=origin/main
```

**The recommended approach is to set the base SHA to the latest successful commit** on the `main` branch. This ensures that all changes since the last successful CI run are accounted for.

{% tabs syncKey="ci-provider" %}
{% tabitem label="GitHub" %}
The [`nrwl/nx-set-shas`](https://github.com/nrwl/nx-set-shas) action uses the GitHub API to find the last successful workflow run on `main` and sets `NX_BASE` and `NX_HEAD`, so `nx affected` needs no extra flags.

```yaml
// .github/workflows/ci.yml
steps:
  - uses: actions/checkout@v7
    with:
      fetch-depth: 0
      filter: tree:0
  - run: npm ci
  - uses: nrwl/nx-set-shas@v5
  - run: npx nx affected -t lint test build
```

`fetch-depth: 0` is required. Without the full Git history, there is no base commit to compare against.
{% /tabitem %}
{% tabitem label="CircleCI" %}
The `nx/set-shas` command from the [Nx Orb](https://github.com/nrwl/nx-orb) finds the last successful pipeline on `main` and sets `NX_BASE` and `NX_HEAD`, so `nx affected` needs no extra flags.

```yaml
// .circleci/config.yml
version: 2.1

orbs:
  nx: nrwl/nx@1.7.0

jobs:
  main:
    docker:
      - image: cimg/node:lts-browsers
    steps:
      - checkout
      - run: npm ci
      - nx/set-shas:
          main-branch-name: 'main'
      - run: npx nx affected -t lint test build
```

{% /tabitem %}
{% tabitem label="GitLab" %}
GitLab has no equivalent action. Set `NX_BASE` and `NX_HEAD` from GitLab's predefined variables instead. Merge request pipelines compare against the merge request's diff base, and pushes to `main` compare against the previous commit on the branch.

```yaml
// .gitlab-ci.yml
image: node:22

CI:
  only:
    - main
    - merge_requests
  script:
    - npm ci
    - export NX_HEAD=$CI_COMMIT_SHA
    - export NX_BASE=${CI_MERGE_REQUEST_DIFF_BASE_SHA:-$CI_COMMIT_BEFORE_SHA}
    - npx nx affected -t lint test build
```

{% /tabitem %}
{% /tabs %}

For other CI providers (Jenkins, Azure Pipelines, Bitbucket), see our guides to get the [last successful CI run for your CI provider](/docs/kb/affected-base-commit).

## Understanding why something is affected

Use the `--explain` flag to show why tasks are affected. Nx prints the explanation, then exits without running anything. Say your branch changes `libs/ui/src/index.ts` instead of the spec file:

```shell
nx affected -t build --explain
```

```text
 NX   2 out of 2 build tasks are affected

Changing 1 file touches 2 build tasks. Pass --verbose to list each with its reasons.

  libs/ui/src/index.ts:
    - ui:build
    - web:build
```

`--explain` explains task selection, so it needs targets passed with `-t`. Without them, or with `affected.legacy` set, it stops with an error.

Pass `--explain=stdout` for the data as JSON, or `--explain=reasons.json` to write it to a file. `nx show projects --affected -t build --explain` works the same way.

## Best paired with remote caching and distribution

`nx affected` can still select a lot of work:

- A change to a project most of the workspace depends on, or to an input every task shares, selects most tasks.
- Every push to a pull request is compared with the same base commit on `main`, so each push selects the same tasks again.

[Remote caching](/docs/features/ci-features/remote-cache) replays the selected tasks whose inputs didn't change since an earlier run, so a later push only runs what its new commits affect.
With [Ultracache](/docs/features/ci-features/remote-cache#ultracache-and-affected), Nx selects tasks from the files each task read in CI instead of its declared inputs, so unused files don't select tasks and reads missing from `inputs` aren't overlooked.
[Distributed task execution](/docs/features/ci-features/distribute-task-execution) spreads the tasks that do run across machines.

## Ignoring files from affected commands

Nx provides two methods to exclude glob patterns (files and folders) from `nx affected -t <targets>`:

- Glob patterns defined in your `.gitignore` file are ignored.
- Glob patterns defined in an optional `.nxignore` file are ignored.

## Lock file changes

By default, a change to your package manager's lock file affects every task.
To limit it to the tasks of projects whose resolved dependencies changed, set [`projectsAffectedByDependencyUpdates`](/docs/reference/nx-json#projectsaffectedbydependencyupdates) to `"auto"` in `nx.json`.

## Reduce affected fan-out for JavaScript and TypeScript projects

If your workspace uses `@nx/js` source analysis, you can also enable dependency narrowing to reduce affected fan-out from source-level imports.

```json
// nx.json
{
  "pluginsConfig": {
    "@nx/js": {
      "dependencyNarrowing": {
        "affectedNarrowing": true
      }
    }
  }
}
```

This is different from `projectsAffectedByDependencyUpdates`.

- `projectsAffectedByDependencyUpdates` controls what happens when the lock file changes.
- `dependencyNarrowing.affectedNarrowing` makes `nx affected` more precise for JavaScript and TypeScript source changes.

See [Narrow project graph dependencies](/docs/technologies/typescript/guides/dependency-narrowing) for the full workflow.

## Not using git

If you aren't using Git, you can pass `--files` to any affected command to indicate what files have been changed.

## Nx 23 and earlier use project-based affected

Nx 23 and earlier use project-based affected: a change to a project runs every task of that project and of every project that depends on it.
Running `nx migrate` to Nx 24 keeps that behavior in existing workspaces by setting `affected.legacy` to `true` in `nx.json`:

```json
// nx.json
{
  "affected": {
    "legacy": true
  }
}
```

Remove the setting, or set it to `false`, to use task-based affected.
`NX_LEGACY_AFFECTED` overrides it for one invocation: `true` uses project-based affected and `false` uses task-based.
Set it for the whole CI job, so `nx show projects --affected -t test` and `nx affected -t test` in the same script see the same affected set.
`nx graph --affected` always shows projects.

